[# Section 4 — identity, hashing, binary wire formats.
   Authored 2026-05-22, expanded with cap-bits + ID/IDREF + FFI lifetime.
   Anchored in spec/canonical.md, spec/identity.md, spec/core/ast-bin.md,
   spec/core/data-bin.md, spec/abi.md. #]

[section id=identity n=4 title='Identity'

  [intro
    """CX's identity story rests on three layers: **canonical bytes**
       (the unique serialization rule that makes two documents
       comparable), **SHA-256 hashing** (the fingerprint computed over
       those bytes), and the **binary wire formats** (ast-bin for AST
       transfer, data-bin for value transfer). This section is the
       working reference; normative depth in
       [`spec/canonical.md`](../../spec/canonical.md),
       [`spec/identity.md`](../../spec/identity.md),
       [`spec/core/ast-bin.md`](../../spec/core/ast-bin.md), and
       [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md)."""]

  [child id=canonical n=4.1 title='Canonical bytes'
    [intro
      """Two CX documents are equal iff their **strict canonical bytes**
         are equal. Canonical bytes are the unique serialization
         produced by `cx canon` — the rules are summarized in
         [[canonical-strict]] and stated normatively in
         [`spec/canonical.md`](../../spec/canonical.md)."""]

    [body
      """Why this matters: every distributed-systems primitive in CX —
         caching, deduplication, content-addressing, cross-binding
         equality — depends on byte-stable canonicalization. Without
         it, two "equal" documents might disagree on hash, on equality,
         or on what bytes get shipped over the wire. CX takes the same
         position as JCS for JSON, XCANON for XML, and the Nix store
         for files: there is one true serialization, and the rules to
         produce it are minimal and total."""]

    [child id=canon-lossless-strict n=4.1.1 title='Lossless vs strict canonical (recap)'
      [body
        """Two canonical forms (see [[canonical-strict]] + [[canonical-lossless]]
           in [[surfaces]] for the surface-level treatment):"""]
      [list
        [item """**Lossless canonical (`cx fmt`)** — preserves comments,
                anchors, aliases, presentation. Idempotent. Used for
                source-file formatting and diff stability."""]
        [item """**Strict canonical (`cx canon` / `cx hash`)** — strips
                comments, expands anchors and aliases, normalizes date
                offsets, sorts attributes lexicographically. Two docs
                with the same data hash identically. Used for content
                addressing."""]]
      [body
        """**The hash is taken over strict canonical bytes**, not over
           lossless. This means `[user email='a' #ada]` and `[user
           #ada email='a']` (different attribute order, same data) hash
           to the same SHA-256."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[# Source: #]
           [user email='ada@example.com' id=42 +active]

           [# cx canon output (strict canonical bytes): #]
           [user active=true email='ada@example.com' id=42]"""]
      [body
        """**Canonicalization rules (strict):**"""]
      [list
        [item """**Attribute key order:** lexicographic by attribute name.
                Ties broken by Unicode code point. Comments stripped."""]
        [item """**String quoting:** single quotes for single-line; triple
                double-quotes for multi-line. Escapes minimal — only
                what the parser requires."""]
        [item """**Whitespace:** no insignificant whitespace; bodies joined
                with single spaces; structural newlines only where the
                grammar requires them."""]
        [item """**Numeric form:** integers as plain digits; floats in the
                shortest representation that round-trips to the same
                value (IEEE-754 shortest-form)."""]
        [item """**Boolean form:** `true` / `false`. Sigil shortcuts
                `+name` / `-name` expand."""]
        [item """**Date/datetime:** offset preserved (NOT normalized to
                UTC); fractional seconds dropped if all zero;
                identity-comparison is by absolute instant."""]
        [item """**Anchors expanded:** `&base` / `*base` shapes resolve
                into their expanded form in the canonical output."""]]]

    [child id=canon-cross-format n=4.1.2 title='Cross-format identity (hash stable through projections)'
      [body
        """The hash is over CX strict canonical bytes — NOT over the
           projection (JSON / YAML / TOML / etc.). This produces a key
           property: **a document's hash is stable through round-trip
           projections**."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """# Original CX
           cx hash users.cx
           # → sha256:3a7bd3e2360a...

           # Convert to JSON then back
           cx convert users.cx --to json > users.json
           cx convert users.json --to cx | cx hash
           # → sha256:3a7bd3e2360a...  (same hash)"""]
      [body
        """The property holds for **lossless** projections (JSON, YAML,
           TOML, XML — within their expressive range; see [[roundtrip]]).
           For **lossy** projections (Markdown, CSV/TSV, JSONL with
           non-trivial nesting) the round-trip drops information; the
           re-imported document will have a different hash. CX makes
           the lossy-ness explicit; see [[roundtrip]] for the full
           matrix."""]
      [body
        """Practical use: in a content-addressable store, you can
           accept incoming data in any supported format, convert to
           CX, hash, dedupe — knowing that two clients sending the
           same data in different formats will produce the same key."""]]]

  [child id=hash n=4.2 title='SHA-256 content hash'
    [intro
      """The identity of a CX document is the SHA-256 of its strict
         canonical bytes. The hash is computed over the UTF-8 byte
         sequence of the canonical form, not over the AST or the
         in-memory value representation."""]

    [example lang=v
      """// V binding — Layer 1
         doc := cx.parse(source)!
         println(doc.hash())   // 'sha256:3a7bd3e2360a...'"""]

    [example lang=python
      """# Python binding — Layer 1
         doc = cx.parse(source)
         print(doc.hash())     # 'sha256:3a7bd3e2360a...'"""]

    [example lang=go
      """// Go binding — Layer 1
         doc, _ := cx.Parse(source)
         fmt.Println(doc.Hash())  // "sha256:3a7bd3e2360a..."""]

    [example lang=rust
      """// Rust binding — Layer 1
         let doc = cx::parse(&source)?;
         println!("{}", doc.hash());  // "sha256:3a7bd3e2360a..."""]

    [child id=hash-stability n=4.2.1 title='Stability across bindings + machines'
      [body
        """The hash function is **stable across bindings, OSes, and
           architectures**. V, Python, Go, and Rust all produce
           byte-identical hash strings for byte-identical input. The
           guarantee extends to architecture (x86-64, ARM64,
           little-endian, big-endian) and OS (macOS, Linux, Windows,
           BSD). Verified by [`conformance/binding_api.txt`](../../conformance/binding_api.txt)
           gate 28.6."""]
      [body
        """**Determinism details:**"""]
      [list
        [item """**Float formatting:** the canonical shortest-form
                produces byte-identical output regardless of host C
                library (`printf("%g")` is NOT used; CX implements its
                own dragon-grisu-style formatter)."""]
        [item """**Endianness:** the canonical bytes are UTF-8 text, no
                multi-byte numeric encoding in the canonical form
                itself."""]
        [item """**Unicode normalization:** identifiers and string
                content are normalized to NFC before canonicalization."""]
        [item """**Locale-independence:** sort order for attribute
                names is by Unicode code point, not by locale collation
                (no LC_COLLATE influence)."""]]]

    [child id=hash-truncation n=4.2.2 title='Truncation safety (short ids)'
      [body
        """SHA-256 is 256 bits (64 hex chars). For human-facing
           identifiers, you can truncate. Birthday-collision math:
           with N items and a hash of B bits, the probability of any
           collision crosses 50% at roughly N ≈ √(π/2 · 2^B). Useful
           safe-truncation lengths:"""]
      [table columns=('truncated to' 'bits' 'safe collection size (1% collision risk)' 'safe (one-in-billion)')
        [row truncated='4 hex (16 bits)' bits=16 safe1='~16' safe1b='very small']
        [row truncated='8 hex (32 bits)' bits=32 safe1='~6,500' safe1b='~200']
        [row truncated='12 hex (48 bits)' bits=48 safe1='~1.7 M' safe1b='~50,000']
        [row truncated='16 hex (64 bits)' bits=64 safe1='~400 M' safe1b='~13 M']
        [row truncated='20 hex (80 bits)' bits=80 safe1='~26 B' safe1b='~860 M']
        [row truncated='24 hex (96 bits)' bits=96 safe1='~1.7 T' safe1b='~55 B']
        [row truncated='full (256 bits)' bits=256 safe1='astronomical' safe1b='astronomical']]
      [body
        """**Recommended:** 16 hex (64 bits) for short IDs in
           UI / URL / log contexts. 24 hex (96 bits) for cross-tenant
           content stores. Full 64 hex for security-sensitive
           identifiers."""]]

    [child id=hash-use-cases n=4.2.3 title='Use cases — cache keys, dedup, content stores'
      [body
        """**Cache key:** `cx hash request.cx` is a stable cache key for
           an expensive computation parameterized on the document.
           Same input → same key → memoization works."""]
      [body
        """**Deduplication:** in a document store, hash on ingestion;
           store the canonical bytes once, point all duplicates at the
           same blob."""]
      [body
        """**Content-addressable storage:** the hash IS the address.
           Git, IPFS, Nix all use the same model. CX hashes interop
           directly:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Git LFS:** point `cx hash`-derived blob ids at the
                CX file's strict-canonical bytes."""]
        [item """**IPFS:** wrap the canonical bytes in a CIDv1 dag-pb
                envelope or use raw codec; the SHA-256 maps to the
                CID's multihash."""]
        [item """**Nix:** identical store-path-shape (SHA-256-named blob
                with metadata sidecar)."""]
        [item """**S3 / object storage:** content-MD5 header is too
                weak; use `x-amz-meta-cx-sha256` for stronger
                integrity."""]]
      [body
        """**Signing:** sign the strict canonical bytes; verify by
           re-canonicalizing on receipt. The signature is robust
           against benign reformatting (whitespace, attribute order,
           comments) because those don't affect strict canonical
           form."""]]]

  [child id=ast-bin n=4.3 title='ast-bin wire format'
    [intro
      """`ast-bin` is the binary serialization of a CX AST. It exists
         for in-process and cross-process transfer of parsed documents
         without re-parsing — bindings ship the AST over the C ABI
         rather than re-tokenizing text. Full spec in
         [`spec/core/ast-bin.md`](../../spec/core/ast-bin.md)."""]

    [body
      """ast-bin is **not** the canonical form. Canonical bytes are CX
         text. ast-bin is the **wire** form between processes that have
         agreed to skip parsing. Two ast-bin payloads can differ
         (different version, different option bits) and still represent
         the same canonical document. The hash is always taken over
         canonical text bytes, never over ast-bin."""]

    [child id=ast-bin-envelope n=4.3.1 title='Buffer envelope'
      [body
        """An ast-bin payload is a self-contained byte buffer with a
           versioned header followed by tree topology and length-prefixed
           atoms. Layout:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Magic + version (8 bytes):** ASCII `'CXAB'` then a
                u32 format version. Reader validates magic + version
                before parsing further."""]
        [item """**Capability bits (8 bytes):** u64 bitmask declaring
                what features the payload uses. A reader that does not
                advertise a required capability refuses the payload."""]
        [item """**Payload length (8 bytes):** u64 byte count of the
                tree-topology section; enables stream chunking."""]
        [item """**Tree topology:** preorder-encoded node tags +
                length-prefixed strings + zig-zag varints for child
                offsets."""]
        [item """**Optional trailer:** SHA-256 of the equivalent
                canonical bytes (skip if the producer doesn't compute
                it) — readers can use it to verify cross-format
                identity without re-canonicalizing."""]]
      [body
        """Length-prefixed strings: all element names, attribute names,
           and string scalars carry their byte length as a varint
           before the bytes. No null terminators."""]]

    [child id=ast-bin-capabilities n=4.3.2 title='Capability bits (full table)'
      [body
        """Capability bits are the forward-compat mechanism. When a
           newer libcx adds a feature, it claims a new cap bit. Readers
           that don't know that bit refuse the payload (rather than
           silently mis-interpret it). The full table:"""]
      [table columns=('bit' 'name' 'meaning' 'introduced')
        [row bit=1  name='schema-driven' meaning='data-bin omits tag bytes; schema is the dictionary' v='v0.5.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=2  name='external-entity' meaning='resolver may load external XML entities' v='v0.5.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=3  name='xml-space-preserve' meaning='xml:space attribute is parser-honored' v='v0.5.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=4  name='include-local' meaning='[?cx include] resolves file-system paths under doc tree' v='v0.5.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=5  name='include-absolute' meaning='[?cx include] resolves absolute paths' v='v0.5.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=6  name='include-network' meaning='[?cx include] resolves HTTPS URLs' v='v0.5.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=7  name='multi-encoding' meaning='document may declare alternate encoding' v='v0.5.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=8  name='doctype-active' meaning='doctype declaration triggers auto-schema-binding' v='reserved for v0.9.x'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=9  name='namespaces' meaning='xmlns: declarations resolved' v='v0.5.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=10 name='cx-lang' meaning='cx:lang scoping honored' v='v0.5.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=11 name='collection-literals' meaning='() [] {} literal forms' v='v0.6.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=12 name='ast-bin-v4' meaning='ast-bin format version 4' v='v0.6.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=13 name='delimited-csv' meaning='CSV / TSV / PSV projections' v='v0.6.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=14 name='diff-tree' meaning='cx diff produces tree-structured diff' v='v0.6.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=15 name='lint-rules' meaning='cx lint rule registry' v='v0.6.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=16 name='streaming-parse' meaning='pull-based streaming parser' v='v0.7.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=17 name='streaming-write' meaning='callback-driven streaming emit' v='v0.7.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=18 name='services' meaning='[?service] / [?http-client] directives' v='v0.7.6'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=19 name='workers' meaning='[?worker] / [?channel] / [?select] directives' v='v0.7.6'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=20 name='async' meaning='[?async] / [?await] / [?await-all/-any/-race]' v='v0.7.6'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=21 name='resilience' meaning='[?retry] / [?timeout] / [?circuit-breaker] / [?fallback] / [?rate-limit] / [?bulkhead]' v='v0.7.6'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=22 name='cxcol-format' meaning='CXCol column-oriented binary format' v='v0.7.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=23 name='arrow-bridge' meaning='Arrow C Data Interface zero-copy bridge' v='v0.7.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=24 name='parquet-bridge' meaning='Parquet read/write via cxlib_arrow' v='v0.7.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=25 name='diagram-renderer' meaning='[?diagram] renders SVG/PNG/Mermaid' v='v0.7.6'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=26 name='thread-register' meaning='cx_thread_register required for cross-thread FFI' v='v0.6.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=27 name='cxpath-value' meaning='CXPath as first-class value kind' v='v0.8.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=28 name='match-multi' meaning='multi-arm [?match] with [case]/[when]/[else] clause children' v='v0.8.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=29 name='modify' meaning='[?modify] directive with action vocabulary' v='v0.8.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=30 name='structural-sharing' meaning='[?modify] uses spine-copy structural sharing' v='v0.8.x'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=31 name='wasm-diagram' meaning='playground wasm exports cx_code_diagram' v='v0.8.0'] [; version-literal-ok ]
        [row bit=32 name='wasm-tree-view' meaning='playground wasm exposes data-tree view C ABI' v='v0.8.0']] [; version-literal-ok ]
      [body
        """Bits 33-63 reserved for future revisions. The cap-bit
           layout is fixed; bits are never re-assigned."""]]

    [child id=ast-bin-versioning n=4.3.3 title='Version negotiation + forward-compat'
      [body
        """When a newer libcx talks to an older binding, the version
           negotiation runs at FFI handshake: `cx_features()` returns
           a u64 bitmask of supported caps. The caller compares with
           the bits required by the payload it's about to send:"""]
      [list
        [item """**All required bits supported:** payload accepted."""]
        [item """**Required bit NOT supported:** payload refused with
                CXER0200 (capability mismatch). Caller MAY downgrade
                — re-emit the payload without the unsupported feature
                if a downgrade path exists — or surface an error to
                the user."""]
        [item """**Unknown bits set:** strict mode refuses; lenient
                mode accepts (the unknown bits are forward-feature
                hints; the payload's required-bits are explicit)."""]]
      [body
        """Lenient mode is the default; strict mode is
           opt-in via `cx_set_strict(true)`. The choice trades
           forward-compat against accidental-misinterpretation risk."""]]]

  [child id=data-bin n=4.4 title='data-bin format'
    [intro
      """`data-bin` is the binary value format for **data** — not parse
         structure. Where ast-bin carries AST nodes (with name / kind /
         child pointers), data-bin carries values (scalars, arrays,
         maps, tables) with optional schema-driven tag omission. Spec:
         [`spec/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md)."""]

    [child id=data-bin-self-describing n=4.4.1 title='Self-describing mode'
      [body
        """Every value is tagged with its kind. Comparable to JSON or
           BSON in flexibility. Used when the schema is not known to
           both sides — e.g., wire-formatted between two CX bindings
           that don't share schemas, or for ad-hoc tooling."""]
      [body
        """Tag bytes: a one-byte type tag (`0x01` = bool, `0x02` = int,
           `0x03` = float, `0x10` = string, `0x20` = array, `0x21` =
           map, etc.) followed by the value bytes. Length prefix where
           applicable. Full tag table in
           [`spec/core/data-bin.md §3`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md)."""]]

    [child id=data-bin-schema-driven n=4.4.2 title='Schema-driven mode (cap bit 1)'
      [body
        """Tag bytes and field names are omitted; the schema is the
           dictionary. Wire size comparable to Protobuf or Avro. Used
           for high-throughput bindings (e.g. the analytics-bridge
           Arrow / Parquet path)."""]
      [body
        """**Negotiation:** the schema-driven mode is gated on
           capability bit 1 (see [[ast-bin-capabilities]]). A reader
           that does not advertise the schema-driven cap bit gets
           self-describing payload, never silently corrupted bytes.
           The schema is exchanged out-of-band (e.g., the `.cxs` file
           is shipped alongside or referenced by hash); the data-bin
           payload carries a SHA-256 of the schema in its trailer so
           readers can verify."""]]

    [child id=data-bin-chunked n=4.4.3 title='Chunked tables (§3.11)'
      [body
        """Tables (`:table` data shape) get a dedicated wire form in
           data-bin §3.11. Layout:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Per-table preamble:** column count, column-spec
                table (name + type + nullability)."""]
        [item """**Per-chunk header:** row count, dictionary references
                if dictionary-encoded."""]
        [item """**Per-column buffers:** contiguous, dictionary-encoded
                where applicable, nullable via a separate validity
                bitmap."""]
        [item """**Per-chunk manifest:** min/max/null-count per column,
                used for predicate pushdown on read."""]
        [item """**Trailer:** cumulative chunk count, file-level
                statistics, optional content-hash of the canonical
                form."""]]
      [body
        """This is the wire format under the CXCol streaming surface
           (see [[cxcol]] in [[analytics]]). Reading is chunk-by-chunk;
           the manifest enables skip-on-predicate without loading the
           full payload."""]]

    [child id=data-bin-compression n=4.4.4 title='Page-compression wrapper (§3.12)'
      [body
        """An optional page-compression wrapper (`0x90` envelope, per
           [`spec/core/data-bin.md §3.12`](../../spec/core/data-bin.md)) wraps
           ranges of bytes inside the payload with a compression
           codec. Supported codecs: Snappy (default), Zstd, LZ4, Gzip.
           The wrapper is gated by cap bit 1 alongside schema-driven
           mode."""]
      [body
        """Compression operates on column buffers, not on the structural
           header — so readers can navigate the page index without
           decompressing the value bodies. The hash is taken over the
           canonical (uncompressed) bytes."""]]]

  [child id=id-anchors n=4.5 title='ID anchors and IDREF resolution'
    [intro
      """The `#name` sigil (see [[id]]) declares a semantic identity on
         an element. Identities are document-scoped and resolvable via
         CXPath `id('name')`. Normative spec:
         [`spec/identity.md`](../../spec/identity.md)."""]

    [child id=id-scoping n=4.5.1 title='Document-unique scoping'
      [body
        """An identity `#name` is unique within a single CX document.
           Duplicate `#name` declarations raise CXER0102 at parse
           time. The uniqueness check spans the entire document
           **after include resolution** — an `[?cx include]`-imported
           sub-document contributes its `#name`s to the same
           namespace."""]
      [body
        """Identities do NOT cross document boundaries except via
           includes. Two separate CX files can each declare `#ada`
           without conflict; they refer to different elements in
           their respective documents."""]]

    [child id=idref-resolution n=4.5.2 title='IDREF resolution + cycles'
      [body
        """A reference to an identity uses the `#name` sigil in
           **value position**: `[post author-id=#ada ...]`. The
           reference resolves to the element bearing `#ada` via
           CXPath's `id('name')` function."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[users
           [user #ada email='ada@example.com']
           [user #grace email='grace@example.com']]
         [posts
           [post author-id=#ada \"\"\"First post\"\"\"]
           [post author-id=#grace \"\"\"Second\"\"\"]]"""]
      [body
        """**Reference cycles** (A references B references A) are not
           an error — the references are values, not container
           relationships; no infinite recursion. However, cyclic
           **merge** references (via the `*` sigil — see [[merge]])
           ARE errors and raise CXER0101."""]
      [body
        """The ID/IDREF model is XML-compatible: round-trip through
           XML preserves the references; round-trip through JSON
           encodes them as `{"$ref": "name"}` envelopes per
           [`spec/conversions.md §4`](../../spec/conversions.md)."""]]

    [child id=id-cross-format n=4.5.3 title='Identity preservation across projections'
      [body
        """IDs survive round-trip through every supported projection:"""]
      [table columns=('format' 'ID encoding' 'IDREF encoding')
        [row format='CX (canonical)' id="#name (on element)" idref='#name (in value position)']
        [row format='XML'           id='id="name"'           idref='attribute value or xlink:href']
        [row format='JSON'          id='"_cx_id": "name"'    idref='{"$ref": "name"} envelope']
        [row format='YAML'          id='&name (anchor)'      idref='*name (alias) — note YAML semantics differ slightly (anchors are syntactic; IDs are semantic)']
        [row format='TOML'          id='id = "name"'         idref='ref = "#name"']
        [row format='ast-bin'       id='attribute on Element' idref='bytes containing #name']]
      [body
        """The data layer preserves the identity; the lossy
           projections (CSV / JSONL with flattening) lose it. For
           those, use CX as the intermediate format."""]]]

  [child id=ffi-lifetime n=4.6 title='FFI lifetime and memory ownership'
    [intro
      """Every binding wraps the libcx C ABI. A Python `Doc` (or Go,
         Rust, V Doc) is a host-language object that points at libcx
         memory. **Who owns the memory? When does it get freed?**
         This section is the developer-facing answer; the normative
         contract is [`spec/abi.md §1.5`](../../spec/abi.md)."""]

    [child id=ffi-doc-lifetime n=4.6.1 title='Doc lifetime per binding'
      [body
        """Every binding's `Doc` value owns a `cx_doc_ref`
           (an opaque libcx handle). The lifetime is:"""]
      [list
        [item """**V:** Boehm-GC-tracked. Dropped when no live reference
                exists; libcx releases the underlying memory at GC
                time. No explicit free."""]
        [item """**Python:** PyObject wrapping a C pointer. Dropped on
                garbage collection (reference count → 0); a
                `__del__`-equivalent finaliser calls
                `cx_doc_release(handle)`."""]
        [item """**Go:** wrapping struct with a
                `runtime.SetFinalizer`-registered cleanup. Dropped
                when the wrapper becomes unreachable; the finaliser
                calls `cx_doc_release(handle)` on the next GC cycle."""]
        [item """**Rust:** wrapping struct with a `Drop` impl. Dropped
                deterministically when the binding goes out of scope;
                the `Drop::drop` calls `cx_doc_release(handle)`."""]]
      [body
        """**Practical implications:**"""]
      [list
        [item """**Long-lived Docs:** keep a reference for the duration
                of use. Bindings do not silently free under pressure."""]
        [item """**Cyclic references:** between two CX Docs are not
                possible (CX values are immutable trees), so no cycle
                detection needed in the host GC."""]
        [item """**Multi-process sharing:** ast-bin is the wire format
                — serialize on one side, deserialize on the other.
                There is no shared-memory Doc passing (yet)."""]]]

    [child id=ffi-node-borrowing n=4.6.2 title='Node borrowing from a Doc'
      [body
        """A `Node` selected from a `Doc` (via `select_all`, `select`,
           `children`, etc.) is a **borrowed reference** into the
           Doc's memory — it does NOT take an independent lifetime.
           When the parent Doc is freed, all Nodes selected from it
           become invalid."""]
      [list
        [item """**Python/Go:** the Node wrapper holds a strong
                reference to the Doc wrapper, preventing the Doc from
                being GC'd while Nodes exist. Safe by construction."""]
        [item """**Rust:** Node carries a lifetime parameter tied to
                the Doc's borrow. The borrow checker prevents use of
                a Node beyond the Doc's lifetime."""]
        [item """**V:** Boehm-GC tracking; the Doc stays live while
                Nodes referencing it are reachable."""]]
      [body
        """**Doc.bytes() output is owned:** the canonical bytes
           returned from `doc.bytes()` are a freshly-allocated copy
           owned by the caller. Modifying or freeing it does not
           affect the Doc."""]
      [body
        """**[?modify] returns a NEW Doc:** the input Doc is unchanged.
           The new Doc has its own lifetime; the input
           Doc continues to exist independently. Structural sharing
           means the two Docs share most of their
           underlying memory but libcx tracks the references so both
           Docs are valid until both are released."""]]

    [child id=ffi-thread-register n=4.6.3 title='Thread registration (cx_thread_register)'
      [body
        """libcx uses Boehm GC, which requires that every thread that
           calls libcx APIs is **registered** with the GC. The C ABI
           exports `cx_init()` (call once per process at start) and
           `cx_thread_register()` (call per thread before first
           libcx call from that thread). Per
           [`spec/abi.md §1.5.5`](../../spec/abi.md)."""]
      [body
        """**Bindings handle this automatically** at the FFI chokepoint
           — every Layer-1 method has a per-thread guard that
           registers the calling thread on first use. Host application
           code doesn't call `cx_thread_register` directly unless
           dropping below the binding layer to raw FFI."""]
      [body
        """**Cap bit 26 (thread-register):** advertised by libcx ≥
           v0.6.0. Older libcx (pre-v0.6.0) ran single-threaded only; [; version-literal-ok ]
           the cap bit's introduction signaled thread-safe FFI."""]
      [body
        """**Practical implication:** spawning a thread from host code
           and calling a CX binding method from it is safe — the
           binding registers the thread transparently. The only
           caveat: if you're calling libcx via raw FFI (not through a
           binding), you must call `cx_thread_register()` yourself
           before any other libcx call from that thread."""]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/concepts/identity.cx,
     docs-src/content/reference/ast-bin.cx, and
     docs-src/content/reference/data-bin.cx during Phase B docs →
     guide consolidation (2026-05-24). Higher-level framing on
     two-layer identity (content-addressed + declarational) and
     compact quick-references for ast-bin / data-bin. #]

  [child id=identity-framing n=4.7 title='Identity framing — two layers + binary forms'
    [intro
      """Identity in CX has two layers. The first is
         content-addressed — every tree has a SHA-256 over its
         canonical bytes; two trees with the same data have the
         same hash. The second is declarational — an element can
         carry an id via `#`, and other places in the document can
         reference it via `@`."""]

    [child id=canonical-recap n=4.7.1 title='Canonical form recap'
      [body
        """Canonical mode strips presentation — comments, anchors,
           whitespace, attribute order normalisation — and emits a
           data-equivalent byte-identical form. Two documents with
           the same data emit to the same bytes; the difference
           between them is something a human added, not something
           the data carries."""]
      [example lang=shell
        """cx canonical menu.cx     # canonical bytes
           cx hash menu.cx          # SHA-256 hex over canonical bytes
           cx eq a.cx b.cx          # exit 0 iff canonical(a) == canonical(b)"""]
      [note
        """Canonical form is the basis for content-addressed
           identity (`cx hash`), for byte-identical conformance
           tests across bindings, and for diff that ignores
           presentation."""]]

    [child id=declared-ids n=4.7.2 title='Declared identifiers (#id, @ref)'
      [body
        """Declared identity is what you write when you want one
           element to point at another by name. Declare with `#`;
           reference with `@`. IDs are unique within their scope;
           the scope is the document root by default."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[order #main
             [item #latte]
             [item #scone]]
           [receipt order=@main]"""]]

    [child id=anchors-merge n=4.7.3 title='Anchors and merge targets'
      [body
        """An anchor (`&` sigil) names a value once and lets later
           places refer to it without repeating it. A merge target
           (`*` sigil) folds the anchor referent into the current
           element. Anchors are anonymous handles; the canonical
           form renumbers them so two documents that bind the same
           data with different anchor names hash to the same
           identity."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[defaults &shared timeout=30 retries=3]
           [server *shared name=api]
           [server *shared name=worker]"""]]

    [child id=ast-bin-quickref n=4.7.4 title='ast-bin quick reference'
      [body
        """ast-bin is the binary serialization of the canonical
           form. The buffer envelope is `[u32 LE size][payload
           bytes]`; payload is the AST in a column-major encoding
           keyed by capability bits. Release the buffer with
           `cx_free`; never call system `free()`. Capability bit
           36 advertises the PathNode wire format (ast_bin v8);
           cap bit 3 advertises symmetric AST
           round-trip. The full bit table is in [`spec/abi.md`
           §3](../../spec/abi.md)."""]]

    [child id=data-bin-quickref n=4.7.5 title='data-bin quick reference (CXCol v1)'
      [body
        """data-bin is the strict-canonical binary data format,
           branded `CXCol` (retitled in commit
           `cbed7754`). Magic bytes are `0x43 0x58 0x44 0x42`
           (historical, preserved for forward-compat); modes are
           self-describing (default), schema-driven (cap bit 1 —
           caller supplies a `.cxs`, payload omits the schema
           preamble), chunked tables (`spec/core/data-bin.md` §3.11),
           and page-compressed (`spec/core/data-bin.md` §3.12). All
           four modes round-trip byte-stable through `cx
           --to=data-bin | cx --from=data-bin`."""]]]
]
